Title:
Thread Hijacking – Local Thread Creation (Classic Thread Context Hijack)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up explains Windows thread fundamentals (scheduling, context switching, user/kernel stacks, TLS, tokens) and then demonstrates classic thread execution hijacking without creating a “new” thread at runtime. The core technique suspends a thread, reads its saved CPU state with `GetThreadContext`, overwrites the instruction pointer (`RIP`) via `SetThreadContext` to point at injected payload memory, and resumes execution. It details how `CONTEXT.ContextFlags` controls which register groups are retrieved/modified and why `CONTEXT_CONTROL` is sufficient for redirecting execution. The post also dives into kernel internals behind `NtGetContextThread`/`NtSetContextThread`, including handle access checks and how registers are sourced from and written back to `_KTRAP_FRAME`. It contrasts hijacking vs `CreateThread`-based execution, emphasizing reduced visibility because no new `_ETHREAD` is created and the original thread start address remains benign. Useful for malware developers, red teamers, and defenders wanting to understand and detect thread-context tampering.

Technical Focus:
- Windows thread context and the `CONTEXT` structure (`ContextFlags`, `RIP/RSP`)
- Thread execution hijacking via `GetThreadContext` / `SetThreadContext` / `ResumeThread`
- Memory allocation and permission transitions (`VirtualAlloc`, `VirtualProtect`)
- Kernel path: `NtGetContextThread`/`NtSetContextThread`, `_ETHREAD/_KTHREAD`, `_KTRAP_FRAME`
- Access rights and handle validation (`THREAD_GET_CONTEXT`, `THREAD_SET_CONTEXT`, `ObReferenceObjectByHandle`)

Use Cases:
- Execute in-process payloads without obvious new-thread creation telemetry
- Build/understand local process injection primitives for loaders
- Develop detections for thread-context manipulation and suspicious `RIP` redirection
- Training material for Windows internals relevant to injection and EDR evasion

Keywords:
thread hijacking, T1055.003, GetThreadContext, SetThreadContext, CONTEXT, ContextFlags, CONTEXT_CONTROL, RIP, RSP, SuspendThread, ResumeThread, CreateThread, CREATE_SUSPENDED, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, NtGetContextThread, NtSetContextThread, _ETHREAD, _KTHREAD, _KTRAP_FRAME, ObReferenceObjectByHandle, THREAD_GET_CONTEXT, THREAD_SET_CONTEXT, KiRestoreContext, trap frame, Windows internals